// The Human Body: the voice, balance, polio and blood donation.
ADDART({id:"voice",t:"The Voice and Speech",c:"Human Body",i:"🗣️",w:"Human voice",r:["lungs","ear","brain","langevo","opera"],
p:[["Vibrating folds","Our voices come from vocal folds vibrating in the throat."],["Shaping sounds","Tongue, lips and teeth turn sound into speech."],["Changing voices","Voices change during puberty and with age."]],
l:"The human voice is the sound produced when air from the lungs passes through the larynx, or voice box, in the throat, making two bands of tissue called vocal folds, or vocal cords, vibrate. The throat, mouth and nose shape this sound, and the tongue, lips, teeth and palate turn it into the vowels and consonants of speech. The voice lets people speak, sing, laugh and shout, and carries emotions, identity and meaning. Each person’s voice is unique, depending on the size and shape of their vocal folds and vocal tract. Voices change during puberty, especially in boys, and with aging. Teachers, singers and others who use their voices a lot must take care to avoid strain and damage.",
s:[["The larynx","The larynx sits at the top of the windpipe and is protected by cartilage, including the thyroid cartilage, which forms the Adam’s apple. Inside are the vocal folds. When we breathe, they open; when we speak, they close and vibrate as air pushes through, opening and closing hundreds of times a second."],["Pitch and volume","Pitch depends on how fast the vocal folds vibrate. Longer, thicker folds vibrate more slowly, producing lower voices, which is why adult men usually have deeper voices than women and children. Tightening the folds raises pitch. Pushing more air through makes the voice louder."],["Shaping speech","The sound from the vocal folds alone is a buzz. The throat, mouth and nose act as resonating chambers, shaping it. Moving the tongue, lips and jaw changes the shape of these chambers, creating different vowels, while blocking or narrowing the airflow creates consonants such as p, t, s and m."],["The brain and speech","Speaking requires precise control by the brain. Areas such as Broca’s area plan speech movements, while Wernicke’s area helps understand language. Damage to these areas, for example from a stroke, can affect a person’s ability to speak or understand."]],
k:[["Voice box","Larynx"],["Sound source","Vocal folds"],["Pitch depends on","Vibration speed"],["Speech area in brain","Broca’s area"],["Common problem","Laryngitis"]],
d:[["1854","Singing teacher Manuel García views his own vocal folds with mirrors"],["1861","Paul Broca identifies a brain area linked to speech"],["1877","Thomas Edison records a human voice on his phonograph"],["1940s","Sound spectrographs make voices visible as patterns"],["1998","Surgeons perform the first full larynx transplant"],["2020s","Artificial intelligence can clone human voices"]],
s2:[["Voice change in puberty","During puberty, hormones cause the larynx to grow and the vocal folds to lengthen and thicken, especially in boys. This lowers the voice, and for a while it may crack or break unexpectedly as the person learns to control the new size. Girls’ voices also deepen slightly. The Adam’s apple becomes more visible in many boys."],["Caring for the voice","Teachers, call center agents, singers and others who use their voices for long hours are at risk of voice strain, hoarseness and nodules on the vocal folds. Experts recommend drinking water, resting the voice, avoiding shouting, using microphones when needed and avoiding smoking. In the Philippines, where many people work in call centers and classrooms, voice care is an important workplace health issue."],["Laryngitis","Laryngitis is inflammation of the larynx, often caused by colds, overuse or irritation, making the voice hoarse or temporarily lost. Most cases go away with rest. Hoarseness lasting more than a few weeks, especially in smokers, should be checked by a doctor, since it can sometimes signal serious problems."],["The singing voice","Singers train to control breathing, pitch and resonance. Classical singers are grouped by voice range, such as soprano, alto, tenor and bass. Some singers can reach very high notes using a special register called the whistle register. Filipinos are known for their love of singing, from choirs and kundiman to karaoke and videoke gatherings."],["Seeing the voice","In 1854 Manuel García, a Spanish singing teacher in London, used a dental mirror and sunlight to observe his own vocal folds while singing, inventing the laryngoscope. Today doctors use small cameras and strobe lights to watch vocal folds vibrate in slow motion and diagnose voice problems."],["Voices and technology","Microphones, telephones, radio and recording changed how voices travel. Today voice assistants recognize speech, and artificial intelligence can create synthetic voices or copy real ones. This helps people who have lost their voices to speak with devices, but voice cloning has also been misused in scams and deepfakes."]],
sp:["The teacher with a mirror","In 1854 Manuel García, a famous Spanish singing teacher working in London, wished he could see what happened inside his throat when he sang. One sunny day in Paris, he placed a small dental mirror at the back of his mouth and held a hand mirror to reflect sunlight onto it. To his amazement, he could see his own vocal folds opening, closing and vibrating as he sang different notes. He presented his observations to the Royal Society the following year. Doctors quickly adopted his method, called laryngoscopy, to examine patients’ throats. García lived to be 101 years old and is honored as a founder of the science of the voice."],
g:[["Larynx","The voice box in the throat"],["Vocal folds","Bands of tissue that vibrate to make sound"],["Pitch","How high or low a sound is"],["Laryngitis","Inflammation of the larynx"]],
f:["Vocal folds vibrate to produce the voice.","Voices deepen during puberty.","The tongue and lips shape speech sounds."],
fun:"The inventor of the laryngoscope first saw his own vocal folds using a dental mirror and sunlight."});

ADDART({id:"balance",t:"Balance and the Inner Ear",c:"Human Body",i:"⚖️",w:"Vestibular system",r:["ear","brain","reflexes","astronauts","eye"],
p:[["A hidden sense","Balance is controlled by tiny organs in the inner ear."],["Feeling motion","Fluid-filled canals sense when the head turns."],["Mixed signals","Confusing signals can make us feel sick on boats and cars."]],
l:"Balance, or equilibrium, is the body’s ability to stay upright and steady while standing still or moving. It depends on the vestibular system, a set of tiny organs in the inner ear that sense head movement and gravity, working together with the eyes and with sensors in muscles and joints that tell the brain about body position. The brain combines these signals and adjusts muscles automatically to keep us balanced. When the signals disagree, people may feel dizzy or experience motion sickness, such as on boats, buses or cars. Problems with the inner ear can cause vertigo, a spinning sensation. Astronauts in space must adapt because the vestibular system no longer senses gravity in the usual way.",
s:[["The vestibular system","Deep inside each inner ear, next to the hearing organ, are three looping semicircular canals filled with fluid, set at right angles to one another. When the head turns, the fluid moves and bends tiny hair cells, signaling the direction and speed of rotation. Two other organs, the utricle and saccule, contain tiny crystals that sense gravity and straight-line movement."],["Eyes and body sensors","Vision tells the brain where the body is in relation to its surroundings, and sensors in muscles, tendons and joints report the position of limbs, a sense called proprioception. Standing on one leg with eyes closed is much harder than with eyes open, because the brain loses visual information."],["Dizziness after spinning","When you spin around and stop suddenly, the fluid in the semicircular canals keeps moving for a moment, so the brain thinks you are still turning. This causes dizziness, and the eyes may flick back and forth. The feeling fades as the fluid settles."],["Motion sickness","Motion sickness happens when the inner ear senses motion that the eyes do not see, or the reverse, such as when reading in a moving car or sitting inside a rocking ship’s cabin. Symptoms include nausea, sweating and vomiting. Looking at the horizon, getting fresh air and sitting where motion is smallest can help."]],
k:[["Main balance organ","Vestibular system in the inner ear"],["Semicircular canals","Three in each ear"],["Gravity sensors","Utricle and saccule"],["Body position sense","Proprioception"],["Spinning sensation","Vertigo"]],
d:[["1861","Prosper Ménière links vertigo to the inner ear"],["1914","Róbert Bárány wins the Nobel Prize for work on the vestibular system"],["1961","Cosmonaut Gherman Titov experiences space sickness"],["1980","John Epley develops a maneuver to treat a common vertigo"],["1990s","Vestibular rehabilitation therapy becomes widely used"],["2020s","Scientists study balance problems in astronauts on long missions"]],
s2:[["Seasickness in an archipelago","In the Philippines, many people travel by ferry and banca between islands, and seasickness is a common experience on rough seas. Travelers are often advised to sit in the middle of the boat, look at the horizon, avoid reading and eat a light meal before the trip. Motion sickness medicines can help but may cause drowsiness."],["Ear crystals and vertigo","The most common cause of vertigo is benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, in which tiny crystals in the inner ear come loose and drift into a semicircular canal. Turning the head or lying down then causes brief spinning spells. In 1980 the American doctor John Epley developed a series of head movements, the Epley maneuver, that guides the crystals back into place, often curing the problem."],["Balance in space","In space, the vestibular organs no longer sense gravity normally, so many astronauts feel disoriented and sick during their first days, a condition called space adaptation syndrome. Their brains gradually adapt. When they return to Earth, they may feel unsteady for days or weeks as they readjust to gravity."],["Balance and aging","As people age, the vestibular system, vision and muscle strength may decline, increasing the risk of falls, a leading cause of injury among older adults. Exercises such as tai chi, walking and strength training can improve balance. Removing tripping hazards at home also helps prevent falls."],["Dancers and spinning","Ballet dancers and figure skaters spin rapidly without getting very dizzy. They use a technique called spotting, fixing their eyes on a point and whipping the head around quickly. Studies suggest that years of training also change how their brains process signals from the inner ear."],["Ménière’s disease","In 1861 the French doctor Prosper Ménière showed that vertigo could come from the inner ear rather than the brain, which most doctors had believed. The condition named after him, Ménière’s disease, causes attacks of vertigo, ringing in the ears and hearing loss, probably due to fluid buildup in the inner ear."]],
sp:["The doctor who moved crystals","In the late 1970s, John Epley, an ear specialist in Portland, Oregon, kept seeing patients with brief, violent spells of spinning whenever they rolled over in bed or looked up. He became convinced that tiny loose crystals were drifting in their inner ear canals. Working with models, he designed a series of slow head turns that would move the crystals out of the canal, like rolling a marble through a maze. Many colleagues were skeptical, and he struggled to get his work published. But patients improved, often after a single treatment. Today the Epley maneuver is a standard, simple treatment for one of the most common causes of dizziness."],
g:[["Vestibular system","The balance organs of the inner ear"],["Semicircular canal","A fluid-filled loop that senses rotation"],["Proprioception","The sense of body position"],["Vertigo","A false feeling of spinning"]],
f:["Balance organs are in the inner ear.","Motion sickness comes from mixed signals.","Astronauts must adapt their balance in space."],
fun:"Dancers avoid dizziness by fixing their eyes on one spot as they spin."});

ADDART({id:"polio",t:"Polio",c:"Health",i:"💉",w:"Polio",r:["vacc","pandemic","measles","who","immune"],
p:[["A paralyzing disease","Polio is a virus that can cause permanent paralysis."],["Nearly eradicated","Vaccines have eliminated polio from most of the world."],["Philippine outbreak","Polio returned to the Philippines briefly in 2019."]],
l:"Polio, or poliomyelitis, is a disease caused by the poliovirus, which spreads mainly through contaminated water and food or contact with an infected person’s feces. Most infections cause no symptoms or only mild illness, but in a small share of cases the virus attacks the nerves of the spinal cord and brain, causing permanent paralysis, usually of the legs, and sometimes death when breathing muscles are affected. Polio mostly affects children under five. In the early 20th century, polio epidemics terrified families worldwide. Vaccines developed in the 1950s and 1960s have reduced cases by more than 99 percent. The Philippines was declared polio-free in 2000, but an outbreak caused by a vaccine-derived virus occurred in 2019, leading to mass vaccination campaigns.",
s:[["How polio spreads","Poliovirus lives in the throat and intestines and is shed in feces. It spreads where sanitation is poor, through contaminated hands, water and food. Infected people without symptoms can still spread it. Clean water, sanitation and handwashing reduce transmission, but vaccination is the main protection."],["Paralysis","In about 1 in 200 infections, the virus invades the nervous system and destroys nerve cells that control muscles, leading to sudden weakness or paralysis. Some people recover partly, but many are left with lifelong disability. Decades later, some survivors develop post-polio syndrome, with new weakness and fatigue."],["Two vaccines","The inactivated polio vaccine, developed by Jonas Salk and introduced in 1955, is given by injection. The oral polio vaccine, developed by Albert Sabin and introduced in the early 1960s, is given as drops by mouth and is cheap and easy to use in mass campaigns. Both have been crucial in fighting polio."],["Vaccine-derived polio","The oral vaccine contains weakened live virus. In communities where too few children are vaccinated, this weakened virus can spread from child to child and, rarely, change into a form that causes paralysis. This is called vaccine-derived polio. The solution is to vaccinate enough children so the virus cannot circulate."]],
k:[["Cause","Poliovirus"],["Mostly affects","Children under five"],["Injected vaccine","Salk, 1955"],["Oral vaccine","Sabin, early 1960s"],["Philippines declared polio-free","2000"]],
d:[["1916","A major polio epidemic strikes New York City"],["1928","The iron lung is introduced to help patients breathe"],["1955","The Salk polio vaccine is declared safe and effective"],["1988","The Global Polio Eradication Initiative is launched"],["2000","The Western Pacific region, including the Philippines, is certified polio-free"],["2019","Polio reappears in the Philippines"]],
s2:[["The 2019 Philippine outbreak","In September 2019, nearly two decades after the country was declared polio-free, the Philippines confirmed cases of vaccine-derived polio in children in Lanao del Sur and other areas. Health officials pointed to low vaccination rates, partly linked to declining public trust in vaccines after controversy over a dengue vaccine. The government, UNICEF and WHO launched large campaigns called Sabayang Patak Kontra Polio, giving oral vaccine to millions of children. The outbreak was declared over in 2021."],["The iron lung","When polio paralyzed the breathing muscles, patients could suffocate. In 1928 engineers Philip Drinker and Louis Agassiz Shaw introduced the iron lung, a large metal tank in which a patient lay with only the head outside, while changing air pressure pushed air in and out of the lungs. In the 1950s, hospital wards were filled with rows of iron lungs."],["Fear in the summer","Before vaccines, polio epidemics often struck in summer in temperate countries. Parents kept children away from swimming pools, beaches and crowds. Polio affected rich and poor alike; US President Franklin D. Roosevelt was paralyzed by an illness diagnosed as polio in 1921. Public fundraising campaigns, such as the March of Dimes, supported research."],["Eradication","In 1988, when the Global Polio Eradication Initiative began, polio paralyzed hundreds of thousands of children each year in over 125 countries. Thanks to vaccination campaigns, wild poliovirus types 2 and 3 have been eradicated, and wild type 1 survives only in Pakistan and Afghanistan. Challenges include conflict, misinformation and reaching every child."],["Salk’s gift","When Jonas Salk’s vaccine was announced as safe and effective on April 12, 1955, church bells rang and people celebrated in the streets. Asked in a television interview who owned the patent, Salk famously replied that there was no patent, comparing it to trying to patent the sun. His words have been widely quoted, though legal experts note that the vaccine might not have been patentable anyway."],["Living with polio","Millions of polio survivors live around the world, some using braces, crutches or wheelchairs. Many have become advocates for disability rights and vaccination. Their experiences remind communities why maintaining high vaccination rates matters even when a disease seems to have disappeared."]],
sp:["Drops for every child","In late 2019, health workers in Mindanao set out with cooler boxes full of vaccine vials. They walked from house to house in villages and city neighborhoods, asking parents if they had children under five. Each child received two drops of oral polio vaccine on the tongue, and a finger was marked with purple ink to show they had been vaccinated. Some parents were hesitant, worried by stories they had heard about vaccines; health workers, local leaders and religious figures explained why the drops were important. Over several rounds, millions of children across the Philippines were vaccinated, and by 2021 the outbreak was declared over."],
g:[["Polio","A disease caused by the poliovirus"],["Paralysis","Loss of the ability to move muscles"],["Eradication","Eliminating a disease worldwide"],["Iron lung","A machine that helped polio patients breathe"]],
f:["Polio can cause permanent paralysis.","The Salk vaccine was introduced in 1955.","Polio reappeared in the Philippines in 2019."],
fun:"When the polio vaccine was announced in 1955, church bells rang across the United States."});

ADDART({id:"blooddonation",t:"Blood Donation",c:"Health",i:"🩸",w:"Blood donation",r:["blood","bloodtypes","dengue","surgery","volunteering"],
p:[["Giving life","Donated blood saves lives in surgery, accidents and illness."],["Safe and quick","Donating takes about an hour, with the blood draw itself only minutes."],["Always needed","Blood cannot be manufactured, so donors are always needed."]],
l:"Blood donation is the voluntary giving of blood so that it can be transfused into patients who need it. Donated blood is used in surgeries, for accident victims, women who bleed heavily during childbirth, people with anemia, cancer patients and those with diseases such as dengue or thalassemia. Blood cannot be manufactured, so hospitals depend on healthy donors. A typical whole-blood donation takes about 450 milliliters, and the body replaces the fluid within a day and the red cells within weeks. Donated blood is tested for infections, sorted by blood type and separated into components such as red cells, plasma and platelets. In the Philippines, the Philippine Red Cross, the Department of Health and hospitals run blood services, and voluntary donation is promoted by law.",
s:[["Who can donate","Donors generally must be healthy adults within age and weight limits set by blood services. In the Philippines, donors are usually between 18 and 65 years old, or 16 and 17 with parental consent, and weigh at least 50 kilograms. Donors are screened with questions and a quick check of blood pressure and hemoglobin to make sure donating is safe for them and the patient."],["The donation process","After registering and screening, the donor sits or lies down while a trained staff member inserts a sterile needle into a vein in the arm. Collection usually takes about 10 minutes. Afterward, donors rest, eat a snack and drink fluids. Mild dizziness can occur, but serious problems are rare."],["Blood components","Donated blood is separated into parts. Red blood cells carry oxygen and are given to patients with blood loss or anemia. Platelets help blood clot and are vital for dengue patients with low platelet counts and cancer patients. Plasma, the liquid part, contains proteins that help clotting and fight infections."],["Matching blood types","Blood must be matched between donor and patient according to the ABO and Rh blood group systems; giving the wrong type can cause dangerous reactions. Type O negative red cells can be given to almost anyone in emergencies, so donors with this type are especially valued."]],
k:[["Typical donation","About 450 mL"],["Collection time","About 10 minutes"],["World Blood Donor Day","June 14"],["Philippine law","National Blood Services Act of 1994"],["Universal red cell donor","Type O negative"]],
d:[["1901","Karl Landsteiner discovers the ABO blood groups"],["1914","Methods to store blood using anticoagulants are developed"],["1937","The first hospital blood bank opens in Chicago"],["1994","The Philippines passes the National Blood Services Act"],["2005","The first World Blood Donor Day is held"],["2025","Australian donor James Harrison, who saved many babies, dies"]],
s2:[["Blood donation in the Philippines","The National Blood Services Act of 1994 promotes voluntary, unpaid blood donation and aims to phase out commercial blood banks. The Philippine Red Cross, Department of Health blood centers and hospitals hold blood drives in schools, offices, malls and churches. Shortages often occur during dengue season, when demand for platelets rises, and during holidays, when fewer people donate."],["Why voluntary donation?","Health organizations, including the World Health Organization, promote voluntary, unpaid donation because volunteers who give for altruistic reasons are less likely to hide health risks than paid donors, making the blood supply safer. In the past, some countries relied on paid donors, which was linked to the spread of infections such as hepatitis."],["The first blood banks","During the early 20th century, scientists learned to store blood by adding chemicals that prevent clotting and keeping it cold. In 1937 Dr. Bernard Fantus opened a blood bank at Cook County Hospital in Chicago, coining the term. During World War II, large-scale blood collection saved many wounded soldiers."],["The man with the golden arm","James Harrison of Australia donated blood plasma more than 1,100 times over about 60 years, beginning in 1954. His plasma contained a rare antibody used to make a treatment that prevents a dangerous condition in which a pregnant mother’s immune system attacks her baby’s blood. His donations are credited with helping protect millions of babies. He died in 2025."],["World Blood Donor Day","Every year on June 14, the birthday of Karl Landsteiner, who discovered blood groups, the world celebrates World Blood Donor Day to thank donors and encourage new ones. Events in the Philippines include blood drives and recognition of regular donors."],["Myths and facts","Some people fear that donating blood will make them weak, cause weight gain or infection. In reality, healthy donors recover quickly, and sterile, single-use needles mean donors cannot catch infections from donating. Experts encourage people to learn the facts and become regular donors if they are eligible."]],
sp:["A blood drive in a school gym","On a Saturday morning, a university gym in Iloilo is filled with rows of beds and volunteers in red vests. Students line up to register, answer health questions and have a finger prick test for hemoglobin. One first-time donor, nervous, holds a friend’s hand as the nurse inserts the needle. Ten minutes later, she is sitting up, eating biscuits and drinking juice, proudly wearing a sticker that says she has donated blood. By the end of the day, the blood bags will be tested, separated and sent to hospitals, where they may help a mother after childbirth, a child with dengue or a patient in surgery."],
g:[["Blood donation","Giving blood to help others"],["Transfusion","Giving blood to a patient"],["Platelets","Blood cells that help clotting"],["Plasma","The liquid part of blood"]],
f:["Blood cannot be manufactured.","World Blood Donor Day is June 14.","Type O negative red cells can be given to almost anyone."],
fun:"One Australian man donated plasma more than 1,100 times over about 60 years."});

;
/* data/earth8.js */
// Planet Earth: the Ring of Fire, Kanlaon Volcano, Earth's layers, and El Niño and La Niña.
ADDART({id:"ringoffire",t:"The Pacific Ring of Fire",c:"Geology",i:"🔥",w:"Ring of Fire",r:["tect","volc","quake","tsunami","pinatubo"],
p:[["A ring of volcanoes","The Ring of Fire is a belt of volcanoes and earthquakes around the Pacific."],["Most active zone","It has most of the world’s active volcanoes and strongest earthquakes."],["The Philippines lies on it","The Philippines sits on the western side of the ring."]],
l:"The Pacific Ring of Fire is a horseshoe-shaped belt about 40,000 kilometers long around the edges of the Pacific Ocean, where many of the world’s volcanoes and earthquakes occur. It stretches from New Zealand and Indonesia through the Philippines, Japan and the Kamchatka Peninsula, across the Aleutian Islands to Alaska, and down the western coasts of North, Central and South America. Roughly three-quarters of Earth’s active volcanoes and about 90 percent of its earthquakes occur along it. The Ring of Fire forms where tectonic plates meet, especially where ocean plates slide beneath other plates in a process called subduction. The Philippines lies on the western part of the ring, which explains its many active volcanoes, such as Mayon, Taal, Pinatubo and Kanlaon, and its frequent earthquakes.",
s:[["Subduction","Most of the Ring of Fire marks subduction zones, where a dense oceanic plate sinks beneath another plate into the mantle. As the sinking plate descends, water released from it lowers the melting point of the surrounding rock, creating magma that rises to form chains of volcanoes. Deep ocean trenches form where the plate bends downward."],["Earthquakes","The movement of plates along subduction zones builds up enormous stress, released suddenly in earthquakes. The largest earthquakes ever recorded, called megathrust earthquakes, happened on the Ring of Fire, such as the 1960 Chile earthquake, magnitude 9.5, and the 2011 Tōhoku earthquake in Japan, magnitude 9.0–9.1."],["Tsunamis","When an undersea earthquake suddenly lifts or drops the seafloor, it can push up the water above, creating tsunamis that cross entire oceans. The Pacific Tsunami Warning Center, set up in 1949, monitors earthquakes and warns countries around the Pacific, including the Philippines."],["Volcanic arcs","Chains of volcanoes called volcanic arcs line the Ring of Fire. Some form island arcs, such as the Aleutians, the Philippines and Japan, while others form mountain ranges on land, such as the Andes and the Cascades. Many of these volcanoes are tall, steep stratovolcanoes that can erupt explosively."]],
k:[["Length","About 40,000 km"],["Share of active volcanoes","About three-quarters"],["Share of earthquakes","About 90 percent"],["Main process","Subduction"],["Largest recorded earthquake","Chile, 1960, magnitude 9.5"]],
d:[["1883","Krakatoa erupts in Indonesia"],["1960","The largest recorded earthquake strikes Chile"],["1980","Mount St. Helens erupts in the United States"],["1991","Mount Pinatubo erupts in the Philippines"],["2011","An earthquake and tsunami strike Japan"],["2022","Hunga Tonga–Hunga Ha’apai erupts in Tonga"]],
s2:[["The Philippines on the ring","The Philippines is caught between the Philippine Sea Plate to the east and the Eurasian Plate and smaller plates to the west. Subduction zones such as the Philippine Trench and the Manila Trench, along with the long Philippine Fault, make the country one of the most seismically active in the world. The Philippine Institute of Volcanology and Seismology, or PHIVOLCS, monitors active volcanoes and earthquakes."],["Living with risk","Around the Ring of Fire, hundreds of millions of people live near active volcanoes or fault lines, often because volcanic soils are fertile and coasts offer good harbors. Countries such as Japan, Chile and the Philippines invest in building codes, warning systems, evacuation drills and public education to reduce disaster risk."],["The 2022 Tonga eruption","In January 2022 the underwater volcano Hunga Tonga–Hunga Ha’apai in the South Pacific erupted with enormous force, sending a plume into the upper atmosphere and creating a pressure wave that circled the globe several times. It triggered tsunamis across the Pacific and injected huge amounts of water vapor into the stratosphere. Tonga was cut off from the world for days when its undersea internet cable was damaged."],["Benefits of volcanoes","Volcanic activity also brings benefits. Volcanic soils are rich in minerals, supporting farming in places such as the slopes of Mayon and in Java. Underground heat provides geothermal energy, and the Philippines is one of the world’s leading producers of geothermal power. Volcanic landscapes attract tourists."],["Not a perfect ring","The Ring of Fire is not a continuous circle. It includes many separate plate boundaries, and some parts, such as the edge of Antarctica, have little activity. Some scientists prefer to call it a belt of plate boundaries rather than a ring."],["Famous eruptions","The 1815 eruption of Tambora in Indonesia, on the Sunda arc often linked to the Ring of Fire, was the deadliest recorded eruption and led to the Year Without a Summer in 1816. The 1991 eruption of Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines was one of the largest of the 20th century and cooled global temperatures slightly for about two years."]],
sp:["The day Pinatubo awoke","For about 500 years, Mount Pinatubo in Zambales had been quiet, covered in forest and home to Aeta communities. In early 1991, steam explosions and earthquakes began. Scientists from PHIVOLCS and the United States Geological Survey installed instruments, studied the volcano’s past eruptions and warned that a large eruption was likely. Tens of thousands of people were evacuated, including from the nearby US Clark Air Base. On June 15, 1991, Pinatubo erupted with massive force, while Typhoon Yunya struck at the same time, mixing ash with rain. Although hundreds died, mostly from roofs collapsing under wet ash, the warnings and evacuations saved many thousands of lives, a lesson for volcano monitoring around the Ring of Fire."],
g:[["Ring of Fire","A belt of volcanoes and earthquakes around the Pacific"],["Subduction","One plate sinking beneath another"],["Volcanic arc","A chain of volcanoes formed by subduction"],["Megathrust earthquake","A very large earthquake at a subduction zone"]],
f:["The Ring of Fire is about 40,000 km long.","Most of the world’s earthquakes occur on it.","The Philippines lies on the Ring of Fire."],
fun:"The pressure wave from the 2022 Tonga eruption circled the Earth several times."});

ADDART({id:"kanlaon",t:"Kanlaon Volcano",c:"Geology",i:"🌋",w:"Kanlaon",r:["volc","taal","mayon","ringoffire","phfolk"],
p:[["Negros’s giant","Kanlaon is the highest mountain in the Visayas."],["Very active","It is one of the most active volcanoes in the Philippines."],["Recent eruptions","Eruptions since 2024 have forced thousands to evacuate."]],
l:"Kanlaon, also spelled Canlaon, is an active stratovolcano on the island of Negros in the central Philippines, rising about 2,435 meters above sea level, making it the highest peak in the Visayas. It lies between the provinces of Negros Occidental and Negros Oriental and is surrounded by forests protected as Mount Kanlaon Natural Park. Kanlaon is one of the most active volcanoes in the country, with frequent ash explosions, and is monitored by PHIVOLCS. An explosive eruption in June 2024 and a larger one in December 2024 led authorities to raise the alert level and evacuate tens of thousands of residents from nearby towns, including Canlaon City and La Castellana. Moderately explosive eruptions continued into 2025 and 2026. The volcano is also sacred in local traditions.",
s:[["The volcano","Kanlaon is a stratovolcano built by layers of lava and ash, with several craters at its summit. Its eruptions are mostly short, explosive bursts of steam and ash, sometimes producing pyroclastic density currents, fast-moving clouds of hot ash and gas, and lahars, mudflows of volcanic material carried by rain."],["Recent activity","On June 3, 2024, Kanlaon erupted explosively, sending an ash plume kilometers into the sky. On December 9, 2024, a stronger eruption sent ash and pyroclastic flows down its slopes, and PHIVOLCS raised the alert to level 3, ordering evacuations within a wide danger zone. The volcano produced further explosive eruptions in 2025. The alert was later lowered to level 2, under which it remained into mid-2026 amid occasional moderately explosive eruptions and ash emissions."],["Effects on communities","Ash fall has damaged crops, closed schools and disrupted flights, and lahars threaten towns along river channels. Evacuees stayed for months in evacuation centers, and farmers lost income from sugarcane, vegetables and livestock. Local governments, the national government and aid groups provided food, shelter and support."],["Mount Kanlaon Natural Park","The forests around Kanlaon are protected as a natural park and are home to rare wildlife, including the Visayan spotted deer, the Visayan warty pig, hornbills and many endemic plants. Before recent eruptions, hikers climbed its trails, though climbing is restricted whenever volcanic activity increases."]],
k:[["Location","Negros Island"],["Height","About 2,435 m"],["Type","Stratovolcano"],["Highest peak in","The Visayas"],["Monitored by","PHIVOLCS"]],
d:[["1866","The earliest recorded eruption of Kanlaon"],["1934","Mount Kanlaon National Park is established"],["1996","A sudden eruption kills hikers near the summit"],["2024","Explosive eruptions in June and December force evacuations"],["2025","Further explosive eruptions occur"],["2026","Kanlaon remains under Alert Level 2 with occasional eruptions"]],
s2:[["The 1996 tragedy","In August 1996, a group of hikers was climbing near Kanlaon’s summit when the volcano suddenly erupted with little warning, showering the area with rocks and ash. Three hikers died and others were injured. The disaster led to stricter rules on climbing active volcanoes and highlighted how dangerous even small eruptions can be."],["Kanlaon in legend","In the traditions of the people of Negros, Kanlaon is linked to Laon, a powerful deity, and the mountain was considered sacred. One legend tells of a hero who defeated a fierce dragon or monster living on the mountain. Some indigenous and local communities still regard the mountain with respect and perform rituals."],["Monitoring Kanlaon","PHIVOLCS watches Kanlaon using seismometers to detect volcanic earthquakes, instruments that measure ground swelling, cameras and gas measurements of sulfur dioxide. Sharp increases in sulfur dioxide or swelling can signal rising magma. Alert levels range from 0 to 5 and tell local governments what actions to take."],["Lahars","After eruptions, ash deposits on the volcano’s slopes can be washed down by heavy rain, forming lahars that flood rivers and bury roads and farms. Lahars from Kanlaon have threatened communities in La Castellana, Canlaon City and other towns, especially during typhoons and the rainy season."],["Living near a volcano","Thousands of families farm the fertile slopes around Kanlaon, growing vegetables, coffee and sugarcane. Many are reluctant to leave their homes and livestock, even during eruptions. Officials work to balance safety with people’s livelihoods, using drills, early warnings and evacuation plans."],["Negros’s other volcanoes","Negros has other volcanic features, including Mount Talinis, also called Cuernos de Negros, near Dumaguete, which has geothermal power plants. The island’s volcanic soils have helped make it the center of the Philippine sugar industry."]],
sp:["The December eruption","On the afternoon of December 9, 2024, residents of towns around Kanlaon heard a roar as the volcano erupted, sending a towering column of ash several kilometers into the sky. Pyroclastic flows rushed down its southern slopes. Within hours, PHIVOLCS raised the alert level to 3, and local officials began evacuating families within the danger zone. Buses and trucks carried people, along with some of their pigs, goats and belongings, to schools and gyms turned into evacuation centers. Ash fell on towns and farms, and some flights were canceled. Many evacuees stayed for weeks or months as the volcano remained restless, waiting for the day they could return home."],
g:[["Stratovolcano","A steep volcano built of layers of lava and ash"],["Pyroclastic flow","A fast cloud of hot ash and gas"],["Lahar","A volcanic mudflow"],["Alert level","A warning level for volcanic activity"]],
f:["Kanlaon is the highest peak in the Visayas.","It erupted explosively in 2024.","PHIVOLCS monitors Kanlaon."],
fun:"Kanlaon’s name is linked to Laon, a powerful deity in the traditions of Negros."});

ADDART({id:"earthlayers",t:"Inside the Earth",c:"Geology",i:"🌍",w:"Structure of Earth",r:["tect","volc","quake","magnet","rocks","pangaea"],
p:[["Layers like an onion","Earth has a crust, mantle, outer core and inner core."],["A hot center","The inner core is about as hot as the Sun’s surface."],["Studied with earthquakes","Scientists learn about Earth’s inside from earthquake waves."]],
l:"Earth is made of layers. The outermost is the crust, a thin shell of rock beneath our feet, between about 5 and 70 kilometers thick. Below it is the mantle, a thick layer of hot rock nearly 2,900 kilometers deep that slowly flows over millions of years. Deeper still is the core, made mostly of iron and nickel: a liquid outer core and a solid inner core with temperatures of around 5,000 degrees Celsius or more, similar to the surface of the Sun. No one has ever drilled beyond the crust; the deepest hole ever drilled reached just over 12 kilometers. Scientists learn about Earth’s interior mainly by studying how earthquake waves travel through it. The movement of the liquid outer core generates Earth’s magnetic field.",
s:[["The crust","There are two types of crust. Oceanic crust, under the oceans, is thin, dense and made mostly of basalt. Continental crust is thicker, lighter and older, made largely of granite-like rocks. The crust and the uppermost mantle together form rigid tectonic plates that move slowly across Earth’s surface."],["The mantle","The mantle makes up most of Earth’s volume. Though made of solid rock, it is so hot and under such pressure that it flows extremely slowly, like very thick putty. Heat rising from the core drives slow currents in the mantle that help move tectonic plates and feed volcanoes."],["The outer core","The outer core is a layer of liquid iron and nickel about 2,200 kilometers thick. As this molten metal swirls, driven by heat and Earth’s rotation, it acts like a giant dynamo, generating Earth’s magnetic field, which protects life from harmful solar particles and guides compasses."],["The inner core","At Earth’s center is the inner core, a solid ball of iron and nickel about 1,220 kilometers in radius. Although it is extremely hot, enormous pressure keeps it solid. It is slowly growing as the outer core cools and freezes onto it."]],
k:[["Layers","Crust, mantle, outer core, inner core"],["Crust thickness","About 5–70 km"],["Depth to core","About 2,900 km"],["Core metals","Iron and nickel"],["Deepest drill hole","About 12.3 km, Kola, Russia"]],
d:[["1909","Andrija Mohorovičić discovers the boundary between crust and mantle"],["1914","Beno Gutenberg calculates the depth of the core"],["1936","Inge Lehmann discovers the solid inner core"],["1989","The Kola Superdeep Borehole reaches about 12.3 km"],["2005","Japan’s drilling ship Chikyu begins operations"],["2023","Scientists report that the inner core’s rotation may have changed"]],
s2:[["Reading earthquake waves","Earthquakes send waves through Earth. P waves can travel through solids and liquids, while S waves cannot pass through liquids. By recording these waves at stations around the world, scientists discovered that the outer core is liquid, because S waves disappear in a shadow zone on the far side of the planet. Changes in wave speed reveal boundaries between layers."],["Inge Lehmann’s discovery","In 1936 the Danish seismologist Inge Lehmann studied waves from a large earthquake in New Zealand and noticed faint waves arriving where none were expected. She concluded that Earth must have a solid inner core inside the liquid outer core, reflecting waves. Her discovery, published in a paper with one of the shortest titles in science, P′, was later confirmed and made her one of the most important geophysicists of the 20th century."],["The deepest hole","From 1970 to the early 1990s, Soviet scientists drilled the Kola Superdeep Borehole in northwestern Russia, reaching a depth of about 12.3 kilometers in 1989, the deepest artificial point on Earth. They found temperatures of about 180 degrees Celsius, much hotter than expected, and water deep in the rock. Still, they had only penetrated about a third of the way through the continental crust."],["Mantle rocks in the Philippines","In some places, pieces of oceanic crust and mantle have been pushed up onto land by tectonic forces, forming rock sequences called ophiolites. The Zambales Ophiolite in Luzon is one of the best-studied examples in the world, allowing Filipino and foreign geologists to examine rocks that formed deep beneath an ancient ocean. These rocks also contain minerals such as chromite and nickel."],["A spinning inner core","The inner core may rotate at a slightly different speed from the rest of the planet. In 2023 Chinese scientists reported evidence that its rotation relative to the mantle had paused and might be reversing, part of a possible cycle lasting decades. Other scientists disagree about how to interpret the data, and research continues."],["Journey to the center","In 1864 the French writer Jules Verne published Journey to the Center of the Earth, imagining explorers descending through volcanic tunnels to a hidden world. In reality, the heat and pressure deep inside Earth make such a journey impossible, but scientists continue to explore the interior using waves, lab experiments and computer models."]],
sp:["A shadow on the far side","In the early 1900s, seismologists around the world began recording earthquakes on new instruments. They noticed something puzzling: when a large earthquake struck, stations on the opposite side of Earth did not receive certain waves, called S waves, which cannot travel through liquids. In 1914 the German scientist Beno Gutenberg calculated that this shadow was caused by a core about 2,900 kilometers below the surface. Later, scientists realized that the outer core must be liquid. In 1936 Inge Lehmann found that faint waves bounced off something solid inside it, revealing the inner core. Without ever visiting, scientists had mapped the heart of the planet using the shaking of earthquakes."],
g:[["Crust","Earth’s thin outer layer"],["Mantle","The thick layer of hot rock beneath the crust"],["Core","Earth’s iron-rich center"],["Ophiolite","A section of oceanic crust and mantle pushed onto land"]],
f:["Earth’s outer core is liquid.","The inner core is solid.","No one has drilled through the crust."],
fun:"The deepest hole ever drilled is only about one-third of the way through the continental crust."});

ADDART({id:"elnino",t:"El Niño and La Niña",c:"Weather",i:"🌊",w:"El Niño–Southern Oscillation",r:["climate","pacific","phtyph","monsoon","rice"],
p:[["Pacific seesaw","El Niño and La Niña are changes in Pacific Ocean temperatures."],["Worldwide weather","They change weather patterns around the world."],["Dry or wet years","El Niño often brings drought to the Philippines, La Niña more rain."]],
l:"El Niño and La Niña are opposite phases of a natural climate pattern called the El Niño–Southern Oscillation, or ENSO, which involves changes in sea surface temperatures and winds across the tropical Pacific Ocean. During El Niño, the central and eastern Pacific become unusually warm, while during La Niña they become unusually cool. These shifts occur every few years and can last from several months to over a year. Because the Pacific is so vast, ENSO affects weather around the world, influencing rainfall, droughts, floods, typhoons, crops and fisheries. In the Philippines, El Niño usually brings below-normal rainfall and drought, harming rice and corn harvests and water supplies, while La Niña tends to bring heavier rains and more tropical cyclones.",
s:[["Normal conditions","Normally, trade winds blow from east to west across the tropical Pacific, pushing warm surface water toward Asia and Australia. Around the Philippines and Indonesia, the warm water fuels clouds and heavy rain. Off South America, cold, nutrient-rich water rises from the deep, supporting rich fisheries."],["El Niño","During El Niño, trade winds weaken or reverse. Warm water spreads eastward toward the Americas. Rain clouds shift east, so places such as the Philippines, Indonesia and Australia often become drier, while Peru and Ecuador may get floods. The upwelling of cold water off South America weakens, harming fisheries."],["La Niña","During La Niña, trade winds become stronger than normal, pushing even more warm water toward Asia. The western Pacific, including the Philippines, often gets heavier rain, flooding and landslides, while parts of South America may face drought. La Niña can also affect the number and paths of typhoons."],["Forecasting ENSO","Scientists monitor ENSO using buoys across the Pacific, satellites and computer models. Weather agencies, including PAGASA in the Philippines, issue El Niño and La Niña watches and alerts months in advance, helping farmers, water managers and governments prepare."]],
k:[["Full name","El Niño–Southern Oscillation"],["El Niño","Warmer eastern Pacific"],["La Niña","Cooler eastern Pacific"],["Typical cycle","Every 2 to 7 years"],["Philippine monitor","PAGASA"]],
d:[["1890s","Peruvian scientists describe the warm current fishermen call El Niño"],["1920s","Gilbert Walker describes the Southern Oscillation"],["1969","Jacob Bjerknes links ocean warming and the Southern Oscillation"],["1997","One of the strongest El Niño events of the century begins"],["2015","A very strong El Niño develops"],["2023","An El Niño begins, contributing to record global heat in 2023 and 2024"]],
s2:[["The name","Fishermen in Peru noticed that warm currents sometimes appeared off their coast around Christmas, reducing fish catches. They called it El Niño, Spanish for the boy child, referring to the infant Jesus. Scientists later used La Niña, meaning the girl, for the opposite cool phase."],["El Niño in the Philippines","Strong El Niño events have caused serious droughts in the Philippines, such as in 1997–1998, 2015–2016 and 2023–2024. Rice and corn fields dried up, dams and reservoirs fell to low levels, causing water shortages in Metro Manila, and some provinces declared states of calamity. The government responds with cloud seeding, irrigation, drought-tolerant crops and aid for farmers."],["La Niña in the Philippines","La Niña years often bring above-normal rainfall, more frequent floods and landslides, and sometimes more typhoons making landfall in the Philippines. The heavy rains can replenish dams but also damage crops and homes. Disaster agencies prepare evacuation plans and warn communities in flood-prone areas."],["Global effects","El Niño tends to raise global average temperatures temporarily, while La Niña tends to lower them. Many of the hottest years on record occurred during or after El Niño events. ENSO also affects Atlantic hurricanes, Indian monsoons, African rainfall and wildfires in Australia and Indonesia."],["Walker’s discovery","In the early 20th century, the British scientist Gilbert Walker, working in India to predict monsoon failures, discovered a seesaw in air pressure between the eastern and western Pacific, which he called the Southern Oscillation. In 1969 the Norwegian-American meteorologist Jacob Bjerknes showed how it was linked to ocean temperatures, explaining El Niño."],["ENSO and climate change","Scientists are studying how global warming may change ENSO. Some studies suggest that extreme El Niño and La Niña events could become more frequent, while others find the evidence uncertain. Even without changes to ENSO itself, warmer temperatures can make the droughts and floods it brings more severe."]],
sp:["Dry fields in Mindanao","In early 2024, during a strong El Niño, farmers in parts of Mindanao and Luzon watched their rice fields crack under the sun. The rains that usually came had failed for months. Irrigation canals ran dry, and some farmers lost entire harvests. In towns and cities, water utilities reduced pressure and scheduled interruptions. Provincial governments declared states of calamity to release emergency funds. Agricultural officials distributed seeds of drought-tolerant crops and encouraged planting vegetables that need less water. When the rains finally returned and El Niño faded later in the year, weather experts were already watching the Pacific for signs of La Niña."],
g:[["El Niño","A warm phase of Pacific Ocean temperatures"],["La Niña","A cool phase of Pacific Ocean temperatures"],["Trade winds","Steady east-to-west tropical winds"],["Upwelling","Cold deep water rising to the surface"]],
f:["El Niño often brings drought to the Philippines.","La Niña often brings heavy rain.","El Niño means the boy child in Spanish."],
fun:"El Niño was named by Peruvian fishermen who noticed the warm current around Christmas."});

;
